in odds near 96 percent"], "rationale": "Pritzker combines incumbency, a dominant financial edge, and a state leaning Democratic, and the available polls and prediction markets all point to a comfortable win. Bailey's cost-and-safety message is a rerun of the 2022 campaign that fell twelve points short, and no current evidence suggests a different outcome.", "watch": "A late scandal, a damaging debate moment, or unusually skewed downstate turnout in a low-salience midterm could narrow the margin, though nothing in the current evidence points that way.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent JB Pritzker (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Illinois' governor's race is a rematch of 2022, when JB Pritzker defeated Darren Bailey by roughly twelve points. Pritzker enters the final weeks with every structural advantage: the incumbency, a dominant fundraising position — outspending Bailey's campaign 25-to-1 — and a state that has trended firmly Democratic. Recent polling shows Pritzker ahead by about twenty points among likely voters, and prediction markets give him roughly a 96 percent chance of winning. Every major race rating calls the contest Solid or Safe Democratic.\n\nBailey has tried to center the campaign on cost of living and public safety, and the two met in a single October debate. But Pritzker is confident enough to have redirected a million dollars to a governor's race in Georgia, and coverage of the contest emphasizes its lack of suspense. The realistic question is the size of Pritzker's winning margin, not which candidate prevails.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.98, "margin": 13.88}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.983, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9753, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9721, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9755, "margin": 13.9}, {"date": "2026-09-29"